# 0001. The setup repo is a Claude Code plugin marketplace

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Records an existing decision (accepted by the owner on 2026-11-11): skills, hooks and MCP
servers for every machine ship as plugins from this repo, installed through Claude Code's
own marketplace mechanism.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the same skills, hooks and MCP servers must work on the Mac and on Arch and
  stay in sync; hand-copied files in `~/.claude` drift per machine and have no history.
- **Marketplace**: `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` names the marketplace `nobodyone` and
  lists every plugin. Own plugins use a relative `source` (`./plugins/workflow`,
  `./plugins/dotfiles`, `./plugins/dev`, `./plugins/engineering`); third-party ones use a
  `github` source pinned by `sha` ([0002](0002-pin-third-party-plugins-by-sha.adr)).
- **Plugin layout**: one plugin per area under `plugins/<name>/`, each with
  `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` (`name`, `version`, `description`, `author`) and its
  `skills/*/SKILL.md`, `hooks/hooks.json` or MCP config.
- **Install**: `make setup` runs `plugins` (`claude plugin marketplace add "$(CURDIR)"`, then
  `claude plugin install <name>@nobodyone` for every plugin read from `marketplace.json`, plus
  the listed official plugins), `lsp`, `browser` and `doctor`. Plugin names come from
  `marketplace.json` itself, so a new plugin needs no Makefile change.
- **Updates**: an installed plugin only changes when its `plugin.json` `version` changes, so
  every PR bumps the version of each plugin it touches (semver) and names it in
  `CHANGELOG.md` (`github-flow`). After a merge: `/plugin marketplace update nobodyone`, then
  `/reload-plugins`.
- **Local source**: the marketplace is added from the local clone (`directory` source), so it
  installs whatever is checked out. The clone stays on `main`; `marketplace-branch.py`
  (`SessionStart` hook) reads `~/.claude/plugins/known_marketplaces.json` and warns, never
  blocks, when a `directory` marketplace is on another branch.
- **Validation**: `make check` runs `claude plugin validate .` and on every `plugins/*/`, plus
  the hook and audit self-tests; CI runs it on every PR and on every update of `main`.
- **Consequences**: one command per machine, history and review through PRs. A forgotten
  version bump means the change never installs; a clone left on a branch installs unmerged
  work (warned at session start).

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Claude Code plugin marketplace and `claude plugin` CLI, GNU Make, GitHub Actions.
- Alternatives: symlink or copy files into `~/.claude`: no versioning, per-machine drift;
  one big plugin: every change reinstalls everything.
